<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest Augustus, Duke of Brunswick</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest Augustus, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, was a grandson of the last King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. His marriage in 1913 to Victoria Louise, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>, reconciled the Hanoverian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welfs</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a> and allowed him to take the throne of Brunswick, held by regents since 1884. He abdicated in the German Revolution of November 1918.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ernest Augustus was born on 17 November 1887 at Penzing near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the youngest of six children of the Hanoverian pretender Ernest Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-king-of-hanover">Duke of Cumberland</a>, and Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thyra">Thyra</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Prussia had deposed his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v-of-hanover">George V of Hanover</a> in 1866. When the reigning Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> died in 1884, the younger Welf line ended and the duchy would by house rules have passed to his father.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-german-empire">Bundesrat</a> rule that the claimant, who had never renounced his claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, would disturb the peace of the empire. Prince Albrecht of Prussia became regent, followed after 1906 by Duke Johann Albrecht of Mecklenburg-Schwerin. An offer by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberlands</a> in 1906 to renounce their claims in favour of the younger Ernest Augustus was rejected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His elder brother George died in a car accident on 20 May 1912, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> sent condolences. The duke sent his surviving son to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> to thank the emperor, and there Ernest Augustus met Wilhelm's only daughter, Princess Victoria Louise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. They married on 24 May 1913 in what was the last great gathering of European sovereigns before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, attended by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a>, Queen Mary and Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>.</p><p>Having sworn allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> and accepted a commission in the Zieten Hussars, he saw his father renounce the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> claim on 27 October 1913. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-german-empire">Bundesrat</a> voted the next day to admit him, and he took possession of the duchy on 1 November. During the war he rose to major-general. In 1917 the Titles Deprivation Act removed his father's British dukedom and his own title as a Prince of the United Kingdom, effective in 1919.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>He was forced to abdicate on 8 November 1918. When his father died in 1923 he did not become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-king-of-hanover">Duke of Cumberland</a> but headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a> for thirty years, living mainly at Blankenburg Castle and Cumberland Castle in Gmunden. He never joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>, but he donated funds and was close to several of its leaders.</p><p>In the mid-1930s Hitler asked the couple to arrange a match between their daughter Frederica and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>; they refused, thinking the age difference too great. Frederica married Prince Paul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1938 and became queen consort in 1947. In 1945 the family moved from Blankenburg to Marienburg Castle in the British zone shortly before Blankenburg passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. He died at Marienburg in 1953 and was buried at Herrenhausen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ernest Augustus, Duke of Brunswick” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When William died in 1884, Prussia blocked the Hanoverian heir, and regents ruled until <b>Ernest Augustus</b> married the Kaiser's daughter Victoria Louise in 1913 and became duke.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-brunswick">Duchy of Brunswick</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Duke Ernest Augustus</b> abdicated on 8 November 1918, and a socialist republic was proclaimed in Brunswick.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-brunswick">Free State of Brunswick</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Penzing, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Pattensen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.19,16.3&amp;year=1929" aria-label="Penzing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ernest Augustus, Duke of Brunswick"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4226 5922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4226 5922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.19,16.3&amp;year=1929">What was here in 1929? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
